## Plan

Step 1: Declare class Student with three private attributes: name, roll and marks.
Step 2: Add a get method, getData(), that reads the three values from the keyboard, and two accessors, getMarks() and getName().
Step 3: In main(), create two objects, call getData() on each, compare the marks and print the name with the higher marks.
Step 4: Handle the tie, so equal marks do not print a wrong name.

## Program

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;
class Student {
string name;
int roll;
float marks;
public:
void getData() {
cout << "Name, roll, marks? ";
cin >> name >> roll >> marks;
}
float getMarks() { return marks; }
string getName() { return name; }
};
int main() {
Student s1, s2;
s1.getData();
s2.getData();
if (s1.getMarks() > s2.getMarks())
cout << s1.getName() << " has the highest marks" << endl;
else if (s2.getMarks() > s1.getMarks())
cout << s2.getName() << " has the highest marks" << endl;
else
cout << "Both have equal marks" << endl;
return 0;
}

## Sample run

Input: Anika 1 78.5 for the first student and Bashir 2 91 for the second.

Name, roll, marks? Anika 1 78.5
Name, roll, marks? Bashir 2 91
Bashir has the highest marks

## Explanation

• The attributes are **private**, so main() can read them only through the public methods (encapsulation).
• getData() is the **get method** the question asks for: it gets input into the object.
• 91 > 78.5, so the second branch runs and prints **Bashir**. With equal marks (e.g. 50 and 50) the program prints "Both have equal marks".

Note: cin >> name reads one word; for a full name with spaces use getline(cin >> ws, name).

Exam tip: examiners give marks for the class with private data, the two objects, the comparison and the output. Write the sample input and output under the code.

