Practice: Model a library with Book, Member and Loan classes. A loan longer than 14 days is fined 5 taka per extra day. Give the report for member Sajib who kept "Data Structures" 10 days and "Operating Systems" 20 days.
import java.util.*;
class Book {
    final String isbn, title;
    Book(String isbn, String title) { this.isbn = isbn; this.title = title; }
}
class Member {
    final int id; final String name;
    Member(int id, String name) { this.id = id; this.name = name; }
}
class Loan {
    final Book book; final Member member; final int days;
    Loan(Book b, Member m, int days) { book = b; member = m; this.days = days; }
    int fine() { return days > 14 ? (days - 14) * 5 : 0; }
}
class Library {
    private final List<Loan> loans = new ArrayList<>();
    void issue(Book b, Member m, int days) { loans.add(new Loan(b, m, days)); }
    void report() {
        for (Loan l : loans)
            System.out.println(l.member.name + " | " + l.book.title + " | fine " + l.fine());
    }
}

Step 1: Loan plays the same role as Assignment: it links a Member to a Book and stores the loan data.
Step 2: 10 days ≤ 14, so the fine is 0.
Step 3: 20 days: (20 − 14) × 5 = 30.

Answer:
Sajib | Data Structures | fine 0
Sajib | Operating Systems | fine 30

